00:00:55.130 --> 00:00:57.350
So summarizing from
the Cloud Spanner 101

00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:00.050
and for all of those of you who
have come to the Cloud Spanner

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:02.380
booth, what is Cloud Spanner?

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:05.150
So Cloud Spanner is Google's
mission-critical scalable

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:07.130
relational database service.

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:09.740
What this means is that you
get a relational database

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:13.700
with ACID semantics and
transactional consistency

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:15.120
with horizontal scalability.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:18.620
So you get the horizontal
scalability of a NoSQL system

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:21.050
with the transactional
semantics and sequence semantics

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:25.180
that you come to expect
from your traditional RDBMS.

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:27.370
Cloud Spanner is a fully
managed database service

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:30.750
with global scale, traditional
relational semantics.

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:35.300
So you get schemas,
ACID transactions, SQL.

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:37.220
We do automatic
synchronous replication

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:39.050
within and across regions.

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:41.330
And it's been battle
tested inside of Google.

00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:43.880
In the 101 session
I went through how

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:47.160
our last manually restart
in MySQL took several years

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:50.030
and led to us to build
Cloud Spanner where it's

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:53.120
been in production at Google
behind most of our mission

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:58.210
critical applications for
the last nine plus years.

00:01:58.210 --> 00:01:59.154
Schema design.

00:01:59.154 --> 00:02:01.570
So one of the things that's
really interesting about Cloud

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:04.540
Spanner is when we did the
demo at the keynote today

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:09.250
and you saw the application you
don't really have many levers.

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:12.160
When you create a Cloud Spanner
instance you give us a name,

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:13.730
you tell us what
the topology is--

00:02:13.730 --> 00:02:16.040
do you want regional and
later this year we'll have

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:17.487
multi-regional--

00:02:17.487 --> 00:02:19.570
and you tell us the number
of nodes that you want.

00:02:19.570 --> 00:02:21.550
You don't pre-provision storage.

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:23.620
You don't tell us
anything about your data.

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:25.660
You just tell us
the number of nodes.

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:31.110
And so 100% of your levers for
optimizing your performance

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:34.870
on Spanner are your
schema and your queries.

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:37.200
So similar to Cloud
Bigtable your schema design

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:40.705
is incredibly important in
determining the performance

00:02:40.705 --> 00:02:43.080
that your application is going
to get when you deploy it.

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:47.760
So let's start with the basics.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:50.600
So people ask me, I've
got a MySQL database,

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:53.720
I've got a postscript
database, how do I migrate?

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:55.610
So the first thing
is your schema.

00:02:55.610 --> 00:02:59.910
Cloud Spanner supports the
most common SQL data types.

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:04.340
So booleans, integers, floats,
strings, bytes, arrays, dates

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:06.350
and timestamps.

00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:08.030
I've put it up
against MySQL so you

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:11.300
can see we support all
of the similar ones

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:13.790
except MySQL doesn't
support arrays.

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:15.770
And then obviously
MySQL and postscripts

00:03:15.770 --> 00:03:17.510
have lots of other
data types things

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:20.470
like JSON and other features
that we haven't implemented yet

00:03:20.470 --> 00:03:23.520
in Cloud Spanner.

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:25.770
The key thing here is that
anything that you see--

00:03:25.770 --> 00:03:28.500
anything that you have in your
MySQL or your postscript schema

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:30.930
should be directly transferable
into Cloud Spanner.

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:38.660
The first thing you have to be
very aware of in Cloud Spanner

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:42.230
when building your schemas
is interleave tables.

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:45.530
So MySQL doesn't have a
concept of child tables.

00:03:45.530 --> 00:03:47.190
Postscripts does
have child tables,

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:49.250
but they're for a
different purpose.

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:52.280
Interleave tables in
Spanner is the way

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:56.600
that you can tell Cloud Spanner
the logical relationship

00:03:56.600 --> 00:03:57.990
of your data.

00:03:57.990 --> 00:04:00.260
And this allows us to
serve your data more

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:02.790
effectively and efficiently.

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:04.670
So the way to think
of an interleave table

00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:07.770
is if you have a row--

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:09.740
in this case singers--

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:12.920
and all of your other
data is related to that--

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:15.050
in this case albums and songs--

00:04:15.050 --> 00:04:17.640
you want them all
joined together.

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:20.690
So think of an interleave
table as a pre-computed

00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:25.990
join on your data to allow your
queries to be really efficient.

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:29.450
What happens here is that when
we are querying your data--

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:33.130
so if you say give me all the
songs for a specific singer--

00:04:33.130 --> 00:04:34.600
if you have those
interleaved then

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:37.030
we can very quickly
and efficiently

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:40.000
return those results
to you because we know

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:41.440
that all the data is related.

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:43.420
And so we'll make best
efforts to make sure

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:46.330
that all of that data is
located on the same node

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:49.540
and very physically close
to its related data.

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:51.610
So we can return it
as quickly as possible

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:53.740
and hopefully avoid
two phase commits

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:55.480
when we do transactions.

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:58.090
So by telling us that
this thing is related

00:04:58.090 --> 00:05:00.835
to this other thing, which
we call data locality,

00:05:00.835 --> 00:05:02.710
we can actually start
to do a much better job

00:05:02.710 --> 00:05:03.585
of serving your data.

00:05:06.917 --> 00:05:09.250
The other thing that's important
about interleave tables

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:12.070
is that Cloud Spanner does
not support the concept

00:05:12.070 --> 00:05:13.900
of foreign key constraints.

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:15.640
Our version of foreign
key constraints

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:17.270
are these interleave tables.

00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:21.160
So if you need cascade
on delete between items

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:23.770
then interleave tables is the
way that you achieve this.

00:05:23.770 --> 00:05:27.400
So if you want to do cascade
on delete for a object

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:29.620
or for a row it's a
pretty good signal to you

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:31.953
that you should probably
interleave it with its parents.

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:38.430
So what does this
look like in a schema?

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:41.730
Again, Cloud Spanner
uses standard SQL

00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:43.620
for schemas and for queries.

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:47.037
So as you're doing your
DDLs in defining your schema

00:05:47.037 --> 00:05:48.370
this is what it would look like.

00:05:48.370 --> 00:05:50.250
So create table singers.

00:05:50.250 --> 00:05:52.560
I have an integer field
for the singer ID.

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:55.620
I've got a string for first
name, a string for last name,

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:59.760
and a byte field for
singer info where

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:02.760
the primary key is singer ID.

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:06.830
Now albums always
are tied to a singer

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:11.180
so therefore I can put a
album ID and an album title,

00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:12.660
set the primary keys.

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:16.200
So on an interleave
table piled tables

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:19.800
have to include the
primary keys of the parent.

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:23.970
This is how we literally do
the physical interleaving

00:06:23.970 --> 00:06:25.860
of the data in the database.

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:28.320
So my primary key has to
include singer ID because

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:29.280
of the parent.

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:32.010
And then I can say
interleave on parent singers

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:34.140
on delete cascade.

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:36.480
So this is how I implement
a foreign key constraint

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:38.430
in terms of cascade on delete.

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:40.620
But it's also how I'd
do a pre-computed join

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:42.830
between singers and albums.

00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:46.540
Now to go another layer songs
always belong to an album.

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:48.790
So now I can say
create table songs.

00:06:48.790 --> 00:06:51.580
We can add a track
ID and a song name.

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:54.100
The primary key has to include
the primary keys of the two

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:56.530
previous tables-- so
Singer ID and album ID--

00:06:56.530 --> 00:06:59.190
and now we can add track
ID as a primary key.

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:02.440
So I interleave and paired
albums for on delete.

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:03.950
So as you're
building your schemas

00:07:03.950 --> 00:07:06.250
this is how you
tell Spanner what

00:07:06.250 --> 00:07:09.430
is the logical relationship
of your data in the schema.

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:16.980
I talked a little bit
about primary keys.

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:20.790
Spanner is different than some
of the relational databases.

00:07:20.790 --> 00:07:23.220
We don't have
auto-increment IDs.

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:27.670
It's very important that we have
distribution of primary keys.

00:07:27.670 --> 00:07:30.750
So it's very important that
you don't do sequential keys,

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:33.610
specifically not timestamps.

00:07:33.610 --> 00:07:36.220
We recommend that you use real
world identifiers because this

00:07:36.220 --> 00:07:38.890
generally gives us sufficient
amount of randomization

00:07:38.890 --> 00:07:41.344
to shard your data
between the nodes.

00:07:41.344 --> 00:07:42.760
And, as we talked
about before, it

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:45.396
defines the physical
relationship of the data.

00:07:45.396 --> 00:07:47.020
So the primary keys
are the way that we

00:07:47.020 --> 00:07:48.670
know that one thing
relates to another.

00:07:52.190 --> 00:07:57.480
The next concept that every good
RDBMS has is secondary indexes.

00:07:57.480 --> 00:08:01.320
So I built my schema,
I've run some queries,

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:03.180
and I see that they're slow.

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:07.170
How can I speed
up those queries?

00:08:07.170 --> 00:08:10.140
One way that I can speed up
a query is by adding indexes.

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:12.720
So Spanner allows you to
build secondary indexes.

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:17.300
So in this case
we've got a table

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:20.360
that has marketing budget,
which is an integer.

00:08:20.360 --> 00:08:23.000
We've defined the primary
keys in the parent table.

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:25.430
And now we've added
a function in our DDL

00:08:25.430 --> 00:08:29.615
that says create index albums
by album title on albums

00:08:29.615 --> 00:08:32.470
where the key is album title.

00:08:32.470 --> 00:08:35.500
So this allows us to-- or
on the column album title.

00:08:35.500 --> 00:08:38.710
So what it does is it allows
us to more efficiently query

00:08:38.710 --> 00:08:40.510
the table albums
without having to do

00:08:40.510 --> 00:08:43.480
a table scan of the
entire primary table.

00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:45.054
Pretty straightforward.

00:08:47.890 --> 00:08:54.250
Interleave indexes allow
you to co-locate your index

00:08:54.250 --> 00:08:55.840
with your data.

00:08:55.840 --> 00:08:58.780
So again, if you are
always doing queries inside

00:08:58.780 --> 00:09:01.960
of a certain key space--
so in this particular time

00:09:01.960 --> 00:09:06.790
if you're always searching for
all the things for a singer--

00:09:06.790 --> 00:09:09.370
I want to know every song
that Prince ever did.

00:09:09.370 --> 00:09:12.550
I want to know every
song that's on an album.

00:09:12.550 --> 00:09:15.730
I want to know if we have
number of records sold

00:09:15.730 --> 00:09:19.300
I want to aggregate all of
the records sold for Prince

00:09:19.300 --> 00:09:21.100
over a period of time.

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:25.540
I can co-locate the index
with the physical data

00:09:25.540 --> 00:09:29.560
to further increase performance
by interleaving that table

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:31.130
in the parent table.

00:09:31.130 --> 00:09:34.030
So I can say create index
songs by singer album

00:09:34.030 --> 00:09:38.590
songs on this table, provide
the columns that I want,

00:09:38.590 --> 00:09:40.430
and interleave it in tables.

00:09:40.430 --> 00:09:43.990
So now when I do that
query there is no chance

00:09:43.990 --> 00:09:47.620
that my index, which is just
a normal table in Spanner,

00:09:47.620 --> 00:09:50.500
doesn't get located
on another node.

00:09:50.500 --> 00:09:53.770
So the way to think about this
is if I build a secondary index

00:09:53.770 --> 00:09:57.820
and I don't tell Cloud Spanner
that it's interleave, what

00:09:57.820 --> 00:10:00.820
can happen is the primary
table could be on one node

00:10:00.820 --> 00:10:03.670
and the index table
could be on another node.

00:10:03.670 --> 00:10:07.960
And now every time I want to
update the primary table I have

00:10:07.960 --> 00:10:12.870
to coordinate between the two of
them and do a two phase commit.

00:10:12.870 --> 00:10:15.870
Similarly if I want to
do a consistent read

00:10:15.870 --> 00:10:18.460
I have to access both
nodes to get the data.

00:10:18.460 --> 00:10:21.210
But if I tell Cloud Spanner
that this data is related

00:10:21.210 --> 00:10:24.690
by interleaving it, then I
can always go to a single node

00:10:24.690 --> 00:10:26.896
and I don't have to
incur the overhead

00:10:26.896 --> 00:10:28.020
of going to multiple nodes.

00:10:36.050 --> 00:10:38.670
Let's talk about
some anti-patterns.

00:10:38.670 --> 00:10:41.081
So I talked a little
bit about this earlier,

00:10:41.081 --> 00:10:43.080
but I wanted to reiterate
the point because it's

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:45.280
a common problem that we see.

00:10:45.280 --> 00:10:48.920
As you're loading data
into Cloud Spanner

00:10:48.920 --> 00:10:57.080
there's a natural inclination
to use timestamps or values that

00:10:57.080 --> 00:11:00.800
will incrementally increase
as you go through the system.

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:02.960
And what happens here is
because you're constantly

00:11:02.960 --> 00:11:05.000
adding keys that
are further along

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:09.050
you're forcing all of the writes
onto a single Cloud Spanner

00:11:09.050 --> 00:11:10.140
server.

00:11:10.140 --> 00:11:12.005
And so what happens
is that you can hot

00:11:12.005 --> 00:11:14.480
spot the server
because you're always

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:16.580
trying to add another value
at the end of the row.

00:11:19.860 --> 00:11:21.370
If you can add a shard ID--

00:11:21.370 --> 00:11:23.770
either by taking a couple
of values off the hash

00:11:23.770 --> 00:11:25.690
or just adding a random shard--

00:11:25.690 --> 00:11:27.520
what that allows us to do--

00:11:27.520 --> 00:11:29.990
and then putting that in
the primary key first--

00:11:29.990 --> 00:11:32.890
what that allows us to do is
to evenly distribute the writes

00:11:32.890 --> 00:11:35.050
across all the Spanner servers.

00:11:35.050 --> 00:11:37.390
So by giving us a
more diverse key space

00:11:37.390 --> 00:11:40.660
you allow us to better shard
the data across the nodes

00:11:40.660 --> 00:11:42.874
and that allows us to more
effectively write data

00:11:42.874 --> 00:11:43.540
into the system.

00:11:47.090 --> 00:11:49.150
One of the things that's
in here is split points.

00:11:49.150 --> 00:11:52.180
So we do our very
best never to split

00:11:52.180 --> 00:11:53.620
between primary
keys and the root

00:11:53.620 --> 00:11:56.950
table, which is the first table
at the top of the interleave.

00:11:56.950 --> 00:11:59.980
And so the more
values you give us

00:11:59.980 --> 00:12:02.470
of primary keys at the
root table the more places

00:12:02.470 --> 00:12:04.930
we have to put splits.

00:12:04.930 --> 00:12:08.080
And because you've told us that
this data isn't interleaved

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:11.330
we know that we can split
it up across multiple nodes

00:12:11.330 --> 00:12:12.757
and that will be fine.

00:12:12.757 --> 00:12:14.590
If you do interleave
it then we know that we

00:12:14.590 --> 00:12:16.475
need to keep it together.

00:12:16.475 --> 00:12:18.100
And that gives us,
again, a better hint

00:12:18.100 --> 00:12:20.847
of how to logically lay out
your data in the database.

00:12:24.050 --> 00:12:26.280
And so where this is most
important is data loading.

00:12:26.280 --> 00:12:28.350
So one of the challenges
is making sure

00:12:28.350 --> 00:12:33.440
that you load in your
data in an efficient way.

00:12:33.440 --> 00:12:36.469
So one is partition your
data by primary key.

00:12:36.469 --> 00:12:38.260
So using the best
practices we talked about

00:12:38.260 --> 00:12:41.440
either invert your
timestamps or put a hash

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:45.440
or create a shard ID in
front of your timestamps

00:12:45.440 --> 00:12:48.952
or moving the timestamp to the
second or third primary key

00:12:48.952 --> 00:12:51.160
is a way to make sure that
we can partition your data

00:12:51.160 --> 00:12:54.580
efficiently and load your
data the most efficient way.

00:12:54.580 --> 00:12:57.790
We also recommend that
you batch your commits

00:12:57.790 --> 00:13:01.450
into 1 to 5 megabyte
mutations at a time

00:13:01.450 --> 00:13:05.020
so you can do multiple writes
in a single transaction.

00:13:05.020 --> 00:13:07.420
We recommend about
1 to 5 megabytes

00:13:07.420 --> 00:13:10.340
of data on each transaction.

00:13:10.340 --> 00:13:15.150
And the last thing is create
your table and your schema,

00:13:15.150 --> 00:13:19.110
load your data, and
then add your indexes.

00:13:19.110 --> 00:13:21.000
It's much faster
to load your data

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:24.210
and then allow us to back
fill a secondary index than it

00:13:24.210 --> 00:13:26.756
is for us to do multiple
writes on every insert

00:13:26.756 --> 00:13:28.380
by making sure that
we've inserted data

00:13:28.380 --> 00:13:32.340
into the primary table and
the index on every write.

00:13:32.340 --> 00:13:33.990
So always add your
indexes at the end

00:13:33.990 --> 00:13:36.950
if you're doing a
large data load job.

00:13:36.950 --> 00:13:39.740
As you saw in the keynote
demo that we did today

00:13:39.740 --> 00:13:45.170
we did an 80 terabyte database
of ticket information.

00:13:45.170 --> 00:13:47.660
And as we were
loading the data, we

00:13:47.660 --> 00:13:50.420
were able to dramatically
increase the load time

00:13:50.420 --> 00:13:53.540
by batching up the
mutations and making sure

00:13:53.540 --> 00:13:55.310
that we had a very
diverse key space.

00:13:55.310 --> 00:13:57.110
And it really does
impact your ability

00:13:57.110 --> 00:13:59.150
to load data into the
system if you break

00:13:59.150 --> 00:14:00.340
any of these best practices.

00:14:03.171 --> 00:14:03.670
All right.

00:14:03.670 --> 00:14:04.750
Query optimization.

00:14:04.750 --> 00:14:07.750
So you have all the basic
pieces of schema designed,

00:14:07.750 --> 00:14:09.430
you understand what
the gotchas are,

00:14:09.430 --> 00:14:11.304
so now you can start to
look at your queries.

00:14:13.425 --> 00:14:18.440
So first of all Cloud SQL speaks
SQL for its schema definition

00:14:18.440 --> 00:14:19.950
and for its querying.

00:14:19.950 --> 00:14:22.170
When you're doing mutations
we provide a separate API

00:14:22.170 --> 00:14:24.960
for doing mutations so that
you can be more explicit to us

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:27.580
about what you want us
to do with your data.

00:14:27.580 --> 00:14:29.730
But as you do your queries
there's a number of ways

00:14:29.730 --> 00:14:31.710
that you can have
slow queries or want

00:14:31.710 --> 00:14:33.280
to speed up your queries.

00:14:33.280 --> 00:14:35.790
So the first one is
missing an index.

00:14:35.790 --> 00:14:38.940
So I want to select last name
first name from singers where

00:14:38.940 --> 00:14:40.860
the last name equals Smith.

00:14:40.860 --> 00:14:45.160
Now the issue here is that I'm
not searching on index data.

00:14:45.160 --> 00:14:46.900
So I'm doing a full table scan.

00:14:46.900 --> 00:14:49.620
So if I want to avoid doing
that I can create an index

00:14:49.620 --> 00:14:53.550
singers by last name on
singers and index last name.

00:14:53.550 --> 00:14:56.610
So this will allow me to more
efficiently query the table

00:14:56.610 --> 00:14:59.060
and pull back that information.

00:14:59.060 --> 00:15:01.360
Now I can actually
make this faster.

00:15:01.360 --> 00:15:04.670
If I go ahead and do
this first query--

00:15:04.670 --> 00:15:08.080
select last name first name
where last name equals Smith--

00:15:08.080 --> 00:15:10.750
I will find the row
based on last name

00:15:10.750 --> 00:15:12.850
but I still have to go
back to the primary table

00:15:12.850 --> 00:15:14.319
to get the first name.

00:15:14.319 --> 00:15:16.360
So I still have to join
back to the primary table

00:15:16.360 --> 00:15:18.050
after I find the key.

00:15:18.050 --> 00:15:20.460
So the way that you can
optimize this in Cloud Spanner

00:15:20.460 --> 00:15:22.790
is using the storing function.

00:15:22.790 --> 00:15:24.250
So you can say
create index singers

00:15:24.250 --> 00:15:27.500
by last name on singer's
last name storing first name.

00:15:27.500 --> 00:15:30.580
So what I've done is I've
added additional information

00:15:30.580 --> 00:15:33.190
to the index so that
I can retrieve data

00:15:33.190 --> 00:15:35.230
by directly reading
the index table

00:15:35.230 --> 00:15:38.370
and never having to go
back to the primary table.

00:15:38.370 --> 00:15:41.060
So again, another way
to alleviate the need

00:15:41.060 --> 00:15:44.510
to go back and do effectively a
join back to the primary table.

00:15:48.670 --> 00:15:50.570
Missing indexes for
ordered results.

00:15:50.570 --> 00:15:54.280
So if you were doing
lots of selects

00:15:54.280 --> 00:15:56.640
and you are doing
order by you want

00:15:56.640 --> 00:15:58.390
to make sure that you
have an index that's

00:15:58.390 --> 00:16:00.212
properly built for that.

00:16:00.212 --> 00:16:02.170
So if you're saying select
first name last name

00:16:02.170 --> 00:16:05.890
from singers order by last
name first name descending,

00:16:05.890 --> 00:16:09.640
then you want to make sure
that you have created an index.

00:16:09.640 --> 00:16:12.280
So singers by last
name on singer's

00:16:12.280 --> 00:16:16.099
last name first
name and descending.

00:16:16.099 --> 00:16:17.890
And the way that you
figure all this out we

00:16:17.890 --> 00:16:19.840
very quickly showed
in the keynote

00:16:19.840 --> 00:16:22.780
today how to look
at your queries.

00:16:22.780 --> 00:16:25.870
And so when you run a
query in the Cloud console

00:16:25.870 --> 00:16:28.380
there's a button there
that says explain query

00:16:28.380 --> 00:16:30.880
and it will show you what we're
actually doing in the query.

00:16:30.880 --> 00:16:32.890
And so these things will
become apparent to you

00:16:32.890 --> 00:16:35.717
as you look at the query plan
to understand whether or not

00:16:35.717 --> 00:16:37.300
you're effectively
using your indexes.

00:16:40.030 --> 00:16:42.010
So what if I'm not
using my indexes?

00:16:42.010 --> 00:16:45.070
Or what if I'm trying to do
different kinds of joins?

00:16:45.070 --> 00:16:47.810
So a number of people
have come up to me today

00:16:47.810 --> 00:16:50.980
at the Spanner booth and
said do you support joins?

00:16:50.980 --> 00:16:51.700
And I say yes.

00:16:51.700 --> 00:16:53.570
And they say which joins?

00:16:53.570 --> 00:16:56.440
And so we do support
a number of joins.

00:16:56.440 --> 00:17:00.970
So inter, cross, full,
left and write we do--

00:17:00.970 --> 00:17:02.920
these are all again
additional vectors for you

00:17:02.920 --> 00:17:05.290
to help optimize how
you're querying your data--

00:17:05.290 --> 00:17:08.147
but we do provide a
number of options for you

00:17:08.147 --> 00:17:10.105
that you would expect
from a traditional RDBMS.

00:17:12.890 --> 00:17:14.900
The other thing that we
do is join directives.

00:17:14.900 --> 00:17:18.319
So we often talk about
how Cloud Spanner uses

00:17:18.319 --> 00:17:21.650
ANSI SQL 2011 with extensions.

00:17:21.650 --> 00:17:25.010
The two main extensions that
we have are interleave--

00:17:25.010 --> 00:17:26.869
so for interleave tables--

00:17:26.869 --> 00:17:29.180
and our annotations.

00:17:29.180 --> 00:17:30.920
And one of the most
common annotations

00:17:30.920 --> 00:17:33.030
is a join directive.

00:17:33.030 --> 00:17:37.190
So you can see in this first
query select star from singers

00:17:37.190 --> 00:17:40.340
join and then this
at bracket annotation

00:17:40.340 --> 00:17:46.590
is what we use across SQL in
Spanner to do annotations.

00:17:46.590 --> 00:17:50.490
Instead of doing a
SQL keyword for this

00:17:50.490 --> 00:17:55.920
we wanted to have a single
semantic for doing these hints,

00:17:55.920 --> 00:17:58.390
not just for joins
but across the data.

00:17:58.390 --> 00:18:00.690
And so this is the
annotation extension

00:18:00.690 --> 00:18:03.457
that we've added to SQL.

00:18:03.457 --> 00:18:05.540
One of the things that's
interesting about Spanner

00:18:05.540 --> 00:18:07.650
and is different than
a traditional RDBMS

00:18:07.650 --> 00:18:10.230
is that Spanner
doesn't try to assume

00:18:10.230 --> 00:18:11.830
too much about your data.

00:18:11.830 --> 00:18:15.940
It doesn't try to figure
out the best possible way

00:18:15.940 --> 00:18:17.750
to run your query.

00:18:17.750 --> 00:18:21.550
We have a query optimizer and if
there's an obvious index for us

00:18:21.550 --> 00:18:23.390
to use then we'll use it.

00:18:23.390 --> 00:18:25.150
But in many scenarios
Spanner will not

00:18:25.150 --> 00:18:27.660
choose to use the index that
you might have intended.

00:18:27.660 --> 00:18:31.210
And so Spanner is designed to
be very explicit about what

00:18:31.210 --> 00:18:32.170
you want to do.

00:18:32.170 --> 00:18:34.390
And so one of these is indexes.

00:18:34.390 --> 00:18:37.630
So when you run this query if
you look on the query explain

00:18:37.630 --> 00:18:40.630
and you notice that you aren't
hitting your join then what you

00:18:40.630 --> 00:18:42.070
can do is use the second query.

00:18:42.070 --> 00:18:43.960
So select singer first
name last name singer

00:18:43.960 --> 00:18:46.720
info from singers at
force index equals

00:18:46.720 --> 00:18:48.470
and you can tell us the index.

00:18:48.470 --> 00:18:50.380
So if you look at your
query explain plan

00:18:50.380 --> 00:18:52.630
and you realize that you
aren't hitting the index

00:18:52.630 --> 00:18:54.340
you can add this
annotation to your join

00:18:54.340 --> 00:18:59.030
to force the query
to use your index.

00:18:59.030 --> 00:19:04.020
And the last one is we also
have additional hash join types.

00:19:04.020 --> 00:19:05.990
So here we've got a hash join.

00:19:05.990 --> 00:19:08.680
There are loop joins as well.

00:19:08.680 --> 00:19:10.550
And all of this is in
the SQL documentation.

00:19:10.550 --> 00:19:14.540
But we do provide all of these
as ways for you to optimize.

00:19:14.540 --> 00:19:17.720
Again, Cloud Spanner the
only knob you get is nodes.

00:19:17.720 --> 00:19:20.370
So all of these tools
that we have in the schema

00:19:20.370 --> 00:19:24.320
and in the querier are your
tools to optimize your queries

00:19:24.320 --> 00:19:27.380
and optimize your performance.

00:19:27.380 --> 00:19:29.740
All write, with that I'm going
to bring up Peter Bakkum.

00:19:29.740 --> 00:19:31.644
Enough of the information
about Spanner.

00:19:31.644 --> 00:19:34.060
I'm sure all of you want to
get some practical information

00:19:34.060 --> 00:19:36.047
about what it really
means to use Spanner,

00:19:36.047 --> 00:19:36.880
so I bring up Peter.

00:19:43.146 --> 00:19:44.990
PETER BAKKUM: Hey
everybody, I'm Peter.

00:19:44.990 --> 00:19:48.230
I work on a platform
at Quizlet and I'm

00:19:48.230 --> 00:19:51.440
going to be talking to
you today about what

00:19:51.440 --> 00:19:53.840
it's like to use a
Spanner from a customer's

00:19:53.840 --> 00:19:58.560
perspective and some of
the results of our testing.

00:19:58.560 --> 00:20:00.780
We were really, really
fortunate to get early access

00:20:00.780 --> 00:20:03.800
to a Spanner and we've been
playing around with it.

00:20:03.800 --> 00:20:08.510
So my goal here is to give you
a really good picture of what

00:20:08.510 --> 00:20:11.210
we've learned and hopefully
come away from this talk

00:20:11.210 --> 00:20:14.210
with a better idea
of how you might

00:20:14.210 --> 00:20:17.591
use Spanner yourself
for your own workloads.

00:20:17.591 --> 00:20:19.340
As I said, we've been
testing for a while.

00:20:19.340 --> 00:20:21.770
I'm going to show you
some of the test results.

00:20:21.770 --> 00:20:24.470
We hope to run Spanner
in production for one

00:20:24.470 --> 00:20:26.040
of our tables very, very soon.

00:20:26.040 --> 00:20:27.950
And we'll have more
information then.

00:20:27.950 --> 00:20:30.800
But I'm going to give you
a complete picture of what

00:20:30.800 --> 00:20:32.940
we know.

00:20:32.940 --> 00:20:37.306
So I want to start by giving you
just a brief introduction to us

00:20:37.306 --> 00:20:39.490
at Quizlet.

00:20:39.490 --> 00:20:40.950
Quizlet is an edtech company.

00:20:40.950 --> 00:20:44.100
We're a study tool.

00:20:44.100 --> 00:20:47.264
The product is you plug
in terms and definitions

00:20:47.264 --> 00:20:49.180
and it gives you different
ways to study them.

00:20:49.180 --> 00:20:52.200
So you can think of it as
like a digital flashcard,

00:20:52.200 --> 00:20:54.576
that kind of thing.

00:20:54.576 --> 00:20:57.180
Quizlet we've been really lucky
to have strong organic growth

00:20:57.180 --> 00:21:00.960
and so Quizlet over time has
grown into a top 25 website

00:21:00.960 --> 00:21:02.370
in the US.

00:21:02.370 --> 00:21:05.520
We have about 25
million users a month

00:21:05.520 --> 00:21:09.420
and a bunch of different
study modes, web clients,

00:21:09.420 --> 00:21:12.720
mobile native
clients, et cetera.

00:21:12.720 --> 00:21:15.900
Our growth curve over
time has looked like this.

00:21:15.900 --> 00:21:20.100
As I said, we've had really
strong organic growth

00:21:20.100 --> 00:21:23.230
and this is an interesting
challenge for databases

00:21:23.230 --> 00:21:24.410
for several reasons.

00:21:24.410 --> 00:21:26.034
So one is that you'll
note is that it's

00:21:26.034 --> 00:21:27.390
very, very seasonal, right?

00:21:27.390 --> 00:21:29.431
It turns out no one really
studies in the summer.

00:21:29.431 --> 00:21:31.550
So we get this big
drop in the summer.

00:21:31.550 --> 00:21:33.060
It's pretty quiet.

00:21:33.060 --> 00:21:35.430
And then around
August, September

00:21:35.430 --> 00:21:36.450
we hit back to school.

00:21:36.450 --> 00:21:39.000
And so there we get
this big, big rush.

00:21:39.000 --> 00:21:42.540
And as we go up that's also
where a lot of our growth hits.

00:21:42.540 --> 00:21:44.880
So in the summer we have
to do is preparation.

00:21:44.880 --> 00:21:46.380
We need to prepare
for this traffic

00:21:46.380 --> 00:21:48.610
that we've never
really seen before.

00:21:48.610 --> 00:21:51.450
So very, very seasonal
but also the traffic

00:21:51.450 --> 00:21:52.620
is gradually increasing.

00:21:52.620 --> 00:21:53.550
It's always going up.

00:21:53.550 --> 00:21:55.050
And so what this
means is that we've

00:21:55.050 --> 00:21:57.480
had to continually
re-architect and re-figure out

00:21:57.480 --> 00:22:02.070
how to run our application.

00:22:02.070 --> 00:22:04.335
Quizlet runs MySQL.

00:22:04.335 --> 00:22:05.960
Like many applications,
we started just

00:22:05.960 --> 00:22:08.420
with a simple web
app using MySQL.

00:22:08.420 --> 00:22:11.120
And over time we've had to
optimize this and grow this

00:22:11.120 --> 00:22:13.190
into a more complex
architecture.

00:22:13.190 --> 00:22:16.760
Our architecture now looks
a little bit like this.

00:22:16.760 --> 00:22:19.550
So let me explain
this a little bit.

00:22:19.550 --> 00:22:21.980
We started with just
a simple database.

00:22:21.980 --> 00:22:24.380
We added replicas
so that you could

00:22:24.380 --> 00:22:27.530
fail-over from the master to a
replica if you needed to you.

00:22:27.530 --> 00:22:30.690
Can also swap back and
forth for maintenance.

00:22:30.690 --> 00:22:32.829
We've added replica for backups.

00:22:32.829 --> 00:22:34.370
And then one of the
ways we've scaled

00:22:34.370 --> 00:22:36.770
is by taking some of our
tables and splitting them out

00:22:36.770 --> 00:22:40.044
into their own pods of
maters and replicas.

00:22:40.044 --> 00:22:41.960
So this is basically
vertical sharding, right?

00:22:41.960 --> 00:22:46.040
We've taken a table, we're
running it separately,

00:22:46.040 --> 00:22:48.230
and we scale up this way.

00:22:48.230 --> 00:22:51.860
So you know this has grown.

00:22:51.860 --> 00:22:55.430
We have about eight pods of
this in production write now.

00:22:55.430 --> 00:22:57.720
You can see on this
diagram some of the pods

00:22:57.720 --> 00:22:59.420
have multiple replicas.

00:22:59.420 --> 00:23:03.320
We also have a tier of
caching to help scale.

00:23:03.320 --> 00:23:05.690
So I think this is a fairly
standard architecture

00:23:05.690 --> 00:23:08.090
and I'm sure that many
people in this room

00:23:08.090 --> 00:23:14.500
have something similar, maybe
even with a greater scale.

00:23:14.500 --> 00:23:15.640
Several problems with this.

00:23:15.640 --> 00:23:18.520
So one is just complexity.

00:23:18.520 --> 00:23:20.680
There's a lot of lines up here.

00:23:20.680 --> 00:23:23.590
It's a complex architecture,
we need to run it,

00:23:23.590 --> 00:23:27.310
we need to handle replication,
backups, et cetera fail-overs

00:23:27.310 --> 00:23:28.840
et cetera.

00:23:28.840 --> 00:23:33.580
So you know maintenance
costs, difficulty.

00:23:33.580 --> 00:23:36.910
But the other problem is
just scalability, right?

00:23:36.910 --> 00:23:39.250
So we haven't done any
horizontal sharding.

00:23:39.250 --> 00:23:40.990
This means that a
single table is only

00:23:40.990 --> 00:23:44.521
running accepting writes at
most on a single machine.

00:23:44.521 --> 00:23:46.770
So this is a pretty fundamental
barrier for us, right?

00:23:49.750 --> 00:23:51.310
We can only handle
so many writes

00:23:51.310 --> 00:23:53.950
on a single machine this means
we're limited in how big we

00:23:53.950 --> 00:23:56.149
can let a table grow.

00:23:56.149 --> 00:23:57.190
So what's the next phase?

00:23:57.190 --> 00:23:59.023
This is really, really
critical for Quizlet.

00:23:59.023 --> 00:24:04.570
This is the primary datastore
so everything depends on this.

00:24:04.570 --> 00:24:06.580
And in fact, if
this goes down we

00:24:06.580 --> 00:24:10.200
literally we have students
complaining to us,

00:24:10.200 --> 00:24:12.430
we have people
tweeting at us, people

00:24:12.430 --> 00:24:16.340
in classrooms come to a halt.
This is what this looks like.

00:24:16.340 --> 00:24:20.140
So if Quizlet's database goes
down we get tweets like this.

00:24:20.140 --> 00:24:22.840
This is literally a
classroom of students.

00:24:22.840 --> 00:24:24.640
It's hard to see but
they're all holding up

00:24:24.640 --> 00:24:26.530
iPads that say Quizlet
is down on them.

00:24:29.370 --> 00:24:31.290
And so I show this just
to underline the point

00:24:31.290 --> 00:24:35.500
that database stability and
scalability is like really,

00:24:35.500 --> 00:24:37.230
really critical for us.

00:24:37.230 --> 00:24:39.670
It's a study product,
it's very high engagement

00:24:39.670 --> 00:24:41.790
when you use it, and so
it's really important

00:24:41.790 --> 00:24:44.940
that the database be
stable in performance

00:24:44.940 --> 00:24:46.870
and it grows over time.

00:24:46.870 --> 00:24:49.860
So we want to
solve this problem.

00:24:49.860 --> 00:24:52.320
We've been examining
Spanner to do that.

00:24:52.320 --> 00:24:55.065
And I want to talk to you next
about how we looked at this

00:24:55.065 --> 00:24:56.940
and how we evaluated
whether Spanner would be

00:24:56.940 --> 00:24:58.231
a good match for this workload.

00:25:00.630 --> 00:25:02.440
So how did do we do this?

00:25:02.440 --> 00:25:05.100
Well, the first thing we did
is we picked a table that we

00:25:05.100 --> 00:25:06.930
were having trouble scaling.

00:25:06.930 --> 00:25:10.770
So first table we picked
was called the Terms table.

00:25:10.770 --> 00:25:14.320
It's about 700 gigabytes
6 billion rows.

00:25:14.320 --> 00:25:17.190
And this is the core
content of Quizlet

00:25:17.190 --> 00:25:20.440
so it's a really important
table for us SQL to scale.

00:25:20.440 --> 00:25:25.290
Receives it's about
70% reads 30% writes.

00:25:25.290 --> 00:25:28.230
So fairly significant
right load.

00:25:28.230 --> 00:25:30.690
So we took this
table, we observed

00:25:30.690 --> 00:25:32.790
how it looked in production.

00:25:32.790 --> 00:25:38.430
So the queries coming in,
did a capture of those.

00:25:38.430 --> 00:25:41.310
Looked in-depth at the
patterns et cetera.

00:25:41.310 --> 00:25:44.680
And then we created a synthetic
workload based on that capture.

00:25:44.680 --> 00:25:47.280
So what I mean by that
is we wrote a script

00:25:47.280 --> 00:25:49.230
to generate queries
in a similar pattern

00:25:49.230 --> 00:25:50.690
as the captured queries.

00:25:50.690 --> 00:25:53.250
So same ratio of
reads and writes,

00:25:53.250 --> 00:25:57.420
same query patterns, same
locality of IDs et cetera

00:25:57.420 --> 00:26:00.390
as close as we can make it look.

00:26:00.390 --> 00:26:01.980
So we had this
synthetic workload

00:26:01.980 --> 00:26:05.190
and then were able to play
this against both Spanner

00:26:05.190 --> 00:26:11.289
in our test environment and
against a separate test MySQL.

00:26:11.289 --> 00:26:13.080
And this is interesting
because it gives us

00:26:13.080 --> 00:26:16.530
a really clean environment for
comparing these two systems

00:26:16.530 --> 00:26:17.980
and doing results.

00:26:17.980 --> 00:26:20.910
So let me caveat this by saying
this is obviously very, very

00:26:20.910 --> 00:26:24.930
specific to our use case and
the results you are going to see

00:26:24.930 --> 00:26:27.560
are totally totally
specific to your workload

00:26:27.560 --> 00:26:29.430
and may look different
in other cases.

00:26:29.430 --> 00:26:31.920
But I want to show you what
this looks like for us.

00:26:36.700 --> 00:26:41.350
First question I have is how
do these databases scale?

00:26:41.350 --> 00:26:43.320
So how did we answer this?

00:26:43.320 --> 00:26:47.530
Well what we've done is we've
taken our synthetic workload,

00:26:47.530 --> 00:26:49.440
we've played against
both systems,

00:26:49.440 --> 00:26:54.210
and we've observed query latency
as we changed the throughput.

00:26:54.210 --> 00:27:00.830
So x-axis here is throughput
y-axis is latency.

00:27:00.830 --> 00:27:05.060
As you can see MySQL latency
initially lower than Spanner.

00:27:05.060 --> 00:27:09.390
So this is around 1 millisecond
during normal conditions.

00:27:09.390 --> 00:27:13.370
We then as you can expect you
eventually hit your performance

00:27:13.370 --> 00:27:16.340
ceiling and so we hit a
threshold at which we really

00:27:16.340 --> 00:27:17.960
can't handle more queries.

00:27:17.960 --> 00:27:22.340
We see a spike in
latency for MySQL here.

00:27:22.340 --> 00:27:27.980
Spanner we have a
higher base latency

00:27:27.980 --> 00:27:30.810
and obviously you also cap
out at a certain point.

00:27:30.810 --> 00:27:34.520
But the key point here is
it's really, really easy

00:27:34.520 --> 00:27:36.860
to just change the
number of nodes

00:27:36.860 --> 00:27:39.590
and scale the Spanner cluster
and move your performance

00:27:39.590 --> 00:27:41.456
ceiling higher.

00:27:41.456 --> 00:27:42.830
So this is the
whole point right?

00:27:42.830 --> 00:27:46.360
This is why this is
a powerful system.

00:27:46.360 --> 00:27:49.230
So this is median latency.

00:27:49.230 --> 00:27:53.810
And the other thing we want
you to look at is tail latency.

00:27:53.810 --> 00:27:58.120
So what you're looking at here
is 99th percentile latency

00:27:58.120 --> 00:28:00.710
on this workload that we ran.

00:28:00.710 --> 00:28:02.700
So again we're comparing
these two systems

00:28:02.700 --> 00:28:05.860
where we're running
Spanner at different sizes.

00:28:05.860 --> 00:28:09.210
And you can see as you approach
your thresholds tail latency

00:28:09.210 --> 00:28:11.696
goes up in this curve, right?

00:28:11.696 --> 00:28:14.470
An interesting
thing here also is

00:28:14.470 --> 00:28:18.100
that the Spanner tail latency
act-- or the MySQL tail latency

00:28:18.100 --> 00:28:22.260
is actually worse than Spanner
for the low query thresholds.

00:28:22.260 --> 00:28:25.470
So again this is
totally specific to us

00:28:25.470 --> 00:28:28.860
and specific to the MySQL
instance we were running,

00:28:28.860 --> 00:28:32.250
but our goal here was to test
the scalability of Spanner

00:28:32.250 --> 00:28:35.280
and how a synthetic workload
would look on the two systems.

00:28:35.280 --> 00:28:38.154
And I think it was a
successful strategy.

00:28:38.154 --> 00:28:39.570
So I want to dig
a little bit more

00:28:39.570 --> 00:28:41.280
into this latency
because I think

00:28:41.280 --> 00:28:44.640
it's really important if you're
using Spanner and implementing

00:28:44.640 --> 00:28:46.720
it to an application.

00:28:46.720 --> 00:28:48.300
So what I'm going
to show you now

00:28:48.300 --> 00:28:51.660
is a histogram of Spanner
latency in this workload.

00:28:51.660 --> 00:28:56.720
So this is Spanner latency
for one of the easier queries

00:28:56.720 --> 00:28:58.190
we were running.

00:28:58.190 --> 00:29:02.990
The axis is buckets of latency
and the y-axis is count.

00:29:02.990 --> 00:29:08.172
So this query on Spanner we saw
5 milliseconds median latency.

00:29:08.172 --> 00:29:09.380
And this is the curve we saw.

00:29:09.380 --> 00:29:13.160
So we see an initial
jump in the curve

00:29:13.160 --> 00:29:16.580
and then we see a
longer tail latency.

00:29:16.580 --> 00:29:21.510
But as I noted before, the
long tail is fairly thin.

00:29:21.510 --> 00:29:25.530
So to us Spanner was
surprisingly consistent

00:29:25.530 --> 00:29:26.705
in its performance.

00:29:32.927 --> 00:29:35.717
So this is great.

00:29:35.717 --> 00:29:36.800
We know about the latency.

00:29:36.800 --> 00:29:40.070
We know a little bit
of a scalability.

00:29:40.070 --> 00:29:44.000
I want to talk more about what
are the implications of Spanner

00:29:44.000 --> 00:29:44.970
for application?

00:29:44.970 --> 00:29:48.344
And one of the other things
we want to know about--

00:29:48.344 --> 00:29:50.230
well, there's several things.

00:29:50.230 --> 00:29:54.560
So one as I noted with Spanner
compared to MySQL just running

00:29:54.560 --> 00:29:57.030
on a vanilla VM
probably your base

00:29:57.030 --> 00:29:59.620
latency is going to be
a little bit higher.

00:29:59.620 --> 00:30:03.020
You tail latency is
probably going to be lower.

00:30:03.020 --> 00:30:04.820
For us a higher
base latency needs

00:30:04.820 --> 00:30:06.702
we need to be more
thoughtful about how

00:30:06.702 --> 00:30:07.910
we use it in the application.

00:30:07.910 --> 00:30:12.340
So asynchronous queries become
a little more important.

00:30:12.340 --> 00:30:15.160
And the other thing is that
it's very, very scalable.

00:30:15.160 --> 00:30:17.940
So again, this is the thing
we really like about Spanner.

00:30:17.940 --> 00:30:18.690
This is the power.

00:30:18.690 --> 00:30:21.000
That we can just change
the number of nodes

00:30:21.000 --> 00:30:23.320
and adjust the performance
ceiling of the database.

00:30:23.320 --> 00:30:25.980
So what this means is that for
your application auto scaling

00:30:25.980 --> 00:30:27.280
becomes really, really simple.

00:30:27.280 --> 00:30:30.890
You're basically just
changing a variable.

00:30:30.890 --> 00:30:32.690
The other thing we noted--

00:30:32.690 --> 00:30:34.340
Spanner is a distributed system.

00:30:34.340 --> 00:30:38.060
As you may know, it partitions
your data into splits.

00:30:38.060 --> 00:30:40.220
And this has implications
on your schema,

00:30:40.220 --> 00:30:43.550
on your IDs, et cetera
as Don mentioned.

00:30:43.550 --> 00:30:46.390
The other interesting
thing we observed--

00:30:46.390 --> 00:30:48.380
so as we were running
the synthetic workload

00:30:48.380 --> 00:30:50.100
we ran it on both systems.

00:30:50.100 --> 00:30:52.040
Both, as you can
imagine, you initially

00:30:52.040 --> 00:30:56.000
start sending the queries
you see warming, right?

00:30:56.000 --> 00:30:57.650
Both systems warm up.

00:30:57.650 --> 00:31:00.780
You see the initial spike and
then they're a bit flatter.

00:31:00.780 --> 00:31:03.580
Cloud Spanner interestingly
also optimizes

00:31:03.580 --> 00:31:06.920
the splits and so over time,
we saw a more gradual warming

00:31:06.920 --> 00:31:08.690
on Spanner.

00:31:08.690 --> 00:31:13.510
So as we ran a
certain cluster size

00:31:13.510 --> 00:31:17.670
the latency would actually
get better over time.

00:31:17.670 --> 00:31:22.970
So we have a good idea now
of Spanner performance,

00:31:22.970 --> 00:31:24.740
scalability, some
of the implications

00:31:24.740 --> 00:31:26.511
of integrating with our app.

00:31:26.511 --> 00:31:28.010
And I want to talk
a little bit more

00:31:28.010 --> 00:31:30.080
about what this
looks like for us.

00:31:30.080 --> 00:31:34.120
In other words, how
we integrate it.

00:31:34.120 --> 00:31:36.080
So several things here.

00:31:36.080 --> 00:31:38.080
First thing is we need
a way to talk to Spanner

00:31:38.080 --> 00:31:41.620
so we need to pick a client.

00:31:41.620 --> 00:31:44.990
Quizlet was originally
written in PHP.

00:31:44.990 --> 00:31:49.660
We now run on HHVM, which
is Facebook's PHP compiler.

00:31:49.660 --> 00:31:53.920
And our code is a
mix of PHP and Hack.

00:31:53.920 --> 00:31:57.410
PHP is the favorite
language of few people.

00:31:57.410 --> 00:32:02.260
And so we've optimized and
we really like HHVM and Hack

00:32:02.260 --> 00:32:05.430
and it's been a good way to
carry our application forward.

00:32:05.430 --> 00:32:09.470
So there's not yet
a Spanner PHP client

00:32:09.470 --> 00:32:12.120
though I understand
one is coming soon.

00:32:12.120 --> 00:32:15.440
So initial options for
the client are one.

00:32:15.440 --> 00:32:16.530
There's the REST API--

00:32:16.530 --> 00:32:18.680
this is just totally
generic sending HTTP

00:32:18.680 --> 00:32:20.780
and getting responses back.

00:32:20.780 --> 00:32:23.660
Two-- one of the things that
I really like about Spanner

00:32:23.660 --> 00:32:27.260
is that it sits behind
a gRCP interface.

00:32:27.260 --> 00:32:29.230
So because it's
gRPC the interface

00:32:29.230 --> 00:32:31.090
is defined totally
programmatically

00:32:31.090 --> 00:32:34.130
and it's actually possible
to generate clients

00:32:34.130 --> 00:32:37.940
in any language
that gRPC supports.

00:32:37.940 --> 00:32:39.890
So we've actually use
both of these strategies

00:32:39.890 --> 00:32:43.150
for our application.

00:32:43.150 --> 00:32:45.490
Next thing for us is how
do we take our application

00:32:45.490 --> 00:32:48.670
and actually create queries to
send to Spanner and send them?

00:32:48.670 --> 00:32:52.300
So like many applications
we use an ORM.

00:32:52.300 --> 00:32:54.830
Ours is custom to us.

00:32:54.830 --> 00:32:57.040
I put an example query here.

00:32:57.040 --> 00:32:59.740
The point is that the
application you're composing

00:32:59.740 --> 00:33:02.860
a query using this
fluent interface,

00:33:02.860 --> 00:33:06.700
you're setting some parameters,
maybe some query predicates,

00:33:06.700 --> 00:33:07.660
et cetera.

00:33:07.660 --> 00:33:09.760
Then you're going
and generating SQL.

00:33:09.760 --> 00:33:14.110
So integration for us looks
like taking this interface

00:33:14.110 --> 00:33:17.380
and reimplementing the back
end specifically for Spanner.

00:33:17.380 --> 00:33:20.140
So the SQL dialect is
a little bit different

00:33:20.140 --> 00:33:25.840
than MySQL and Spanner
doesn't offer DML statements.

00:33:25.840 --> 00:33:28.060
So that's a little
bit different.

00:33:28.060 --> 00:33:31.520
But in general
it's very similar.

00:33:31.520 --> 00:33:33.010
And this is the
whole point, right?

00:33:33.010 --> 00:33:36.370
We want something
relational we can move to.

00:33:36.370 --> 00:33:38.410
So we've taken
this ORM interface

00:33:38.410 --> 00:33:41.392
and we've reimplemented it for
Spanner and so within our app

00:33:41.392 --> 00:33:43.600
we're now able to communicate
with both systems using

00:33:43.600 --> 00:33:44.946
the same fluent interface.

00:33:44.946 --> 00:33:46.570
This is our strategy
for handling this.

00:33:50.146 --> 00:33:52.880
So I want to talk a little bit
more about schema for our case.

00:33:56.330 --> 00:33:59.510
As Dom, mentioned there's some
definite schema implications

00:33:59.510 --> 00:34:01.680
for Spanner.

00:34:01.680 --> 00:34:05.210
You know the nice part is
that much of our schema maps

00:34:05.210 --> 00:34:06.080
directly over.

00:34:06.080 --> 00:34:08.060
Again, this is the whole point.

00:34:08.060 --> 00:34:11.630
We want to move
to a system where

00:34:11.630 --> 00:34:14.840
we have the relational model,
our data types carry over,

00:34:14.840 --> 00:34:19.199
our queries carry over,
and it's more scalable.

00:34:19.199 --> 00:34:21.350
So most things
map directly over.

00:34:21.350 --> 00:34:23.510
There's a few implications
for the ID format.

00:34:23.510 --> 00:34:25.524
I'm going to dive into that.

00:34:25.524 --> 00:34:27.440
And then the other
important thing to remember

00:34:27.440 --> 00:34:32.449
is just you want your schema
to be thoughtful around data

00:34:32.449 --> 00:34:33.500
locality.

00:34:33.500 --> 00:34:37.130
So you want to use your schema
to limit the number of queries

00:34:37.130 --> 00:34:38.449
that touch multiple splits.

00:34:38.449 --> 00:34:42.520
In other words, that touch a
bunch of areas of your data.

00:34:42.520 --> 00:34:44.558
And I'll show you
how we do this.

00:34:44.558 --> 00:34:49.480
So I'm going to show
you our schema on MySQL

00:34:49.480 --> 00:34:51.479
and then our schema on Spanner.

00:34:51.479 --> 00:34:52.520
There's a lot of details.

00:34:52.520 --> 00:34:54.645
I'm going to highlight the
important parts of this.

00:34:57.480 --> 00:34:59.560
It's mostly pretty
similar, right?

00:34:59.560 --> 00:35:03.940
We've taken our columns,
we've mapped them over,

00:35:03.940 --> 00:35:06.910
we've mapped them into
the Spanner's data types.

00:35:06.910 --> 00:35:09.430
The things I'm going to
call out here are one--

00:35:09.430 --> 00:35:11.230
we've added new ID columns.

00:35:11.230 --> 00:35:16.920
So we've added columns
specific to Spanner's ID.

00:35:16.920 --> 00:35:21.970
Sorry, specific to Spanner's
favorable primary key

00:35:21.970 --> 00:35:23.590
characteristics.

00:35:23.590 --> 00:35:26.010
We've changed the primary key.

00:35:26.010 --> 00:35:29.790
And we've intentionally
used a primary key

00:35:29.790 --> 00:35:33.390
that gives us a locality
on that column Spanner ID.

00:35:33.390 --> 00:35:36.570
So the Spanner ID
column there's locality

00:35:36.570 --> 00:35:37.840
within the Spanner set ID.

00:35:37.840 --> 00:35:40.710
And we've designed the
primary key that way.

00:35:40.710 --> 00:35:42.570
We've also leveraged
a secondary index here

00:35:42.570 --> 00:35:45.672
and so we're creating that
in the scheme as well.

00:35:45.672 --> 00:35:47.130
So let me explain
a little bit more

00:35:47.130 --> 00:35:50.400
about how we're handling IDs.

00:35:50.400 --> 00:35:53.580
So, as Don mentioned,
Spanner does not

00:35:53.580 --> 00:35:57.750
have auto incrementing IDs and
even if it did you probably

00:35:57.750 --> 00:35:59.680
wouldn't want to use them.

00:35:59.680 --> 00:36:01.950
The reason is that you want
to distribute your writes

00:36:01.950 --> 00:36:04.920
over multiple splits
and so it's much more

00:36:04.920 --> 00:36:07.710
favorable to use something
with a lot of entropy

00:36:07.710 --> 00:36:09.420
in the first few bits.

00:36:09.420 --> 00:36:12.360
So there's different
ways to slice this.

00:36:12.360 --> 00:36:15.180
Our choice was v4 uuid
and this is basically

00:36:15.180 --> 00:36:18.450
just randomly generated uuid.

00:36:18.450 --> 00:36:22.087
There's other uuid formats v1
that aren't favorable for this

00:36:22.087 --> 00:36:24.420
and the reason is that they
can include a time component

00:36:24.420 --> 00:36:26.086
in the very beginning
so you're not even

00:36:26.086 --> 00:36:27.330
redistributing your rights.

00:36:27.330 --> 00:36:29.370
So we don't store any
meaning in the IDs.

00:36:29.370 --> 00:36:33.500
So we're just using
randomly generated IDs.

00:36:33.500 --> 00:36:35.750
The other interesting
thing in our case

00:36:35.750 --> 00:36:38.240
is that backwards
compatibility with previous IDs

00:36:38.240 --> 00:36:39.750
is very important to us.

00:36:39.750 --> 00:36:44.210
So as I mentioned, Quizlet
has native mobile clients.

00:36:44.210 --> 00:36:46.040
All these native
mobile clients they

00:36:46.040 --> 00:36:48.800
depend on a certain
version of the API.

00:36:48.800 --> 00:36:51.890
They access models with
certain assumptions

00:36:51.890 --> 00:36:53.659
like they have integer IDs.

00:36:53.659 --> 00:36:55.200
And they actually
cache data as well.

00:36:55.200 --> 00:36:58.460
So they're storing
data and saving them.

00:36:58.460 --> 00:37:04.910
And so migrating to a new system
means supporting those clients

00:37:04.910 --> 00:37:07.640
because they're mobile clients
we can't just upgrade them

00:37:07.640 --> 00:37:08.840
all immediately, right?

00:37:08.840 --> 00:37:10.680
They're out there in the wild.

00:37:10.680 --> 00:37:15.560
So we very explicitly
left in our previous IDs

00:37:15.560 --> 00:37:19.010
and we have a secondary index
in our schema for those IDs.

00:37:19.010 --> 00:37:22.010
This is a little bit--

00:37:22.010 --> 00:37:25.170
this is a diagram of
what this looks like.

00:37:25.170 --> 00:37:27.080
We have multiple
versions of our API.

00:37:27.080 --> 00:37:31.762
We've planned a new version
to support a new ID scheme

00:37:31.762 --> 00:37:34.220
and we're going to be migrating
mobile clients over to this

00:37:34.220 --> 00:37:34.740
as we go.

00:37:40.560 --> 00:37:44.260
One more thing I want
to talk about and that's

00:37:44.260 --> 00:37:46.730
how we move data into Spanner.

00:37:46.730 --> 00:37:50.480
So, as I mentioned, uptime
is very important to us.

00:37:50.480 --> 00:37:51.770
Stability is important to us.

00:37:51.770 --> 00:37:55.430
And so we can't just shut
the site down for hours

00:37:55.430 --> 00:37:58.160
as we prepare the new
database and copy data in.

00:37:58.160 --> 00:38:01.160
So we want to be able to
transition over, flip a switch,

00:38:01.160 --> 00:38:03.250
and have it be done.

00:38:03.250 --> 00:38:08.720
So the way we think about
this is replicating data

00:38:08.720 --> 00:38:10.860
by establishing writes
to both systems.

00:38:10.860 --> 00:38:13.820
So we take our application,
we're using MySQL,

00:38:13.820 --> 00:38:15.770
we're reading and
writing for MySQL,

00:38:15.770 --> 00:38:18.712
MySQL is a source of truth.

00:38:18.712 --> 00:38:20.420
We're also duplicating
writes to Spanner.

00:38:20.420 --> 00:38:22.910
So we send writes there as well.

00:38:22.910 --> 00:38:27.044
That means mutations to our
models go to both systems.

00:38:27.044 --> 00:38:28.460
While that's
happening we then run

00:38:28.460 --> 00:38:30.410
a copy client in the
background that copies

00:38:30.410 --> 00:38:34.490
data over into Spanner
and eventually you

00:38:34.490 --> 00:38:37.040
end up with a very
consistent data set.

00:38:42.030 --> 00:38:45.600
This is a little tricky
because only one system can

00:38:45.600 --> 00:38:46.990
be the source of truth, right?

00:38:50.110 --> 00:38:51.940
Only one system can
be the source of truth

00:38:51.940 --> 00:38:53.694
and so if a write
fails for the other one

00:38:53.694 --> 00:38:54.610
you need to know that.

00:38:54.610 --> 00:38:56.990
You then have a
data inconsistency.

00:38:56.990 --> 00:39:00.940
So for us this just means
tracking those failed writes

00:39:00.940 --> 00:39:04.540
and ensuring that both
systems are consistent.

00:39:04.540 --> 00:39:08.320
So the whole point of this
is to transition over easily,

00:39:08.320 --> 00:39:10.030
so you begin writing
in both systems,

00:39:10.030 --> 00:39:11.680
you do the copy
in the background,

00:39:11.680 --> 00:39:14.920
you reach a point where they're
similar, you stop writes,

00:39:14.920 --> 00:39:17.870
and then you can just flip
over to the new system.

00:39:17.870 --> 00:39:21.200
This is our plan for switching
over a table to Spanner.

00:39:21.200 --> 00:39:24.660
So that's all I've prepared.

00:39:24.660 --> 00:39:25.630
Thanks for listening.

00:39:25.630 --> 00:39:26.610
I hope this was useful.

00:39:26.610 --> 00:39:30.260
[MUSIC PLAYING]

